// THE ANSWER TO THE T001 SPEC §5 SECOND FLAG, PINNED (shared-v1 / T002 u2).
//
// The spec asks: can frameless `shared` scoping JOIN SEPARATELY-MOUNTED LIFTED
// PARTS without demo-layer wiring? The lift prints one module per Base UI part,
// so a `<C.Provider>` lowering lands in one `.tsrx` and every consumer of that
// context lands in another. The spec makes the answer a GATE: if it is no, that
// is a language-level need and T002 stops rather than working around it.
//
// u2 measured the answer as NO on three independent grounds and pinned all
// three. u3 then FLIPPED two of them in place, in `packages/compiler/src`, under
// the T001 supplemental ruling: wall 1 (a declaration-only module is refused)
// and wall 3 (a renamed cross-module read has an unmapped property) were
// frameless integration defects and are now closed, each with its fail-closed
// negative control beside it. The pins were edited, never deleted: the flip is
// the oracle.
//
// What did NOT move is as load-bearing as what did. Wall 2 (markless's
// helper-state interception of the PLAIN named import) still refuses with
// MARKLESS_STATE_HELPER_RETURN_UNSUPPORTED, because markless was not touched and
// the vendored tarball was not bumped. Wall 3b (the namespace spelling) still
// yields nothing at all. Wall 4 (the provider is a separate export the host must
// mount) still stands and is composition-by-placement, not a defect.
//
// It is a MEASUREMENT PIN, not an aspiration: every assertion below records what
// the toolchain does TODAY. When any of them starts doing something else, this
// lane goes red and someone re-reads the gate.

const PROBE_FILE = 'lift/generated/probe/lift-shared-consumer.tsrx';

describe('shared scoping across separately-mounted lifted parts', () => {
	// WALL 1 - FLIPPED BY u3. A declaration with no component of its own now
	// COMPILES: component discovery accepts zero components exactly when the
	// markless graph carries at least one `shared` definition, and the enriched
	// IR carries `components: []` plus the definition. This is the module spec
	// §1 names ("Entry with accepted S1 and NO M4 host row (MenuRoot) prints a
	// declaration-only module"), so the shape is now printable.
	test('a declaration-only module compiles with zero components and its definition', async () => {
		const result = await probe(
			'declaration only',
			'lift/generated/probe/lift-shared-decl.tsrx',
			DECLARATION_ONLY,
		);
		expect(result.error).toBeNull();
		expect(result.components).toEqual([]);
		expect(result.sharedDefinitions).toEqual([{ name: 'usePair', scope: 'container' }]);
	});

	// THE NEGATIVE CONTROL FOR THAT RELAXATION, and the reason it is a relaxation
	// rather than a deletion: with no `shared` definition to carry either, a
	// module with no component is still refused, in the identical sentence.
	test('a module with neither a component nor a shared definition is still refused', async () => {
		const result = await probe(
			'nothing at all',
			'lift/generated/probe/lift-shared-empty.tsrx',
			'export const NOT_A_COMPONENT = 1;\n',
		);
		expect(result.error).toBe('TSRX component discovery found no top-level component functions.');
	});

	// WALL 2. The natural cross-module spelling - a plain named import of the
	// factory - never reaches the shared-instance path at all. Markless routes
	// any `const x = importedFromTsrx()` inside a component through its
	// cross-module HELPER-STATE path first, and that path errors without module
	// graph interface data, which `@frameless/compiler` does not supply.
	test('a plain named cross-module import is refused by markless as helper state', async () => {
		const result = await probe('cross-module consumer', PROBE_FILE, CROSS_MODULE_CONSUMER);
		expect(result.error).toContain('MARKLESS_STATE_HELPER_RETURN_UNSUPPORTED');
		expect(result.error).toContain('Cannot call imported helper "usePair"');
